'''turtle-deck''' (tėr′tl-dek), ''n.'' See ''deck'', 2.

'''turtle-dove''' (tėr′tl-duv), ''n.'' [< ME. ''turteldoufe'' = D. ''tortelduif'' = OHG. ''turtulatūbā'', ''turtiltūbā'', MHG. ''turteltūbe'', ''türteltūbe'', G. ''turteltaube'' = Dan. ''turteldue'' = Sw. ''turturdufva''; as ''turtle''<sup>1</sup> + ''dove''<sup>1</sup>.] '''1.''' The turtle; any member of the genus ''Turtur'' in a broad sense; specifically, ''T. vulgaris'', a dove native in the British Islands
[[FIGURE]]
{{c|{{smaller|Turtle-dove (''Turtur vulgaris'').}}}}
and other parts of Europe, and thence extending into Africa and Asia. {{smaller|There are many others, of most parts of the Old World, as the Cambayan, ''T. senegalensis''; among them is ''T. risorius'', commonly seen in captivity and called ''ring-dove''.}}

